Fix FlitAppender, so that Serialize{REQ,RSP,DAT,SNP} produce a correct flit.

Three defects, all in Append32, and together they mean serialization has never
worked -- the assert in Finish() fires on the first flit. Only deserialization
was ever tested: it arrived in RISMicroDevices/CHIron#1, the one human-authored
pull request the repository has merged.

  1. `offset` is never advanced for a field that does not cross a word
     boundary. There is no `else` branch. Every such field is therefore written
     on top of the previous one at the same bit position.

  2. `index` is advanced twice when a field does cross, once by the `++index`
     inside the subscript and once by the statement after it, so the writer
     skips a whole 32-bit word.

  3. `0xFFFFFFFFU >> (32 - rem)` is undefined behaviour when `rem == 0`, which
     is every field that ends exactly on a word boundary.

The masking of `value` moves ahead of both uses so the spill shifted into the
next word is masked too, and the `width == 0` guard covers the optional fields
whose width the configuration can set to zero -- DataCheck, Poison and MPAM.

Worth sending upstream.

--- a/include/chi/util/chi_util_flit.hpp	2026-08-19 10:04:00
+++ b/include/chi/util/chi_util_flit.hpp	2026-08-19 10:04:00
@@ -484,16 +484,30 @@
 
                 inline void Append32(uint32_t value, size_t width) noexcept
                 {
-                    flitBits[index] |= (value & (0xFFFFFFFFU >> (32 - width))) << offset;
+                    if (width == 0)
+                        return;
 
+                    value &= (width >= 32) ? 0xFFFFFFFFU : ((UINT32_C(1) << width) - 1);
+
+                    flitBits[index] |= value << offset;
+
                     if (offset + width >= 32)
                     {
                         size_t rem = offset + width - 32;
 
-                        flitBits[++index] = (value >> (32 - offset)) & (0xFFFFFFFFU >> (32 - rem));
-
-                        ++index;
+                        // Assignment rather than |=, so that the word the next field
+                        // starts in is initialised exactly once. This is the only path
+                        // that advances `index`, so every word is reached by it -- and
+                        // the caller must supply one word of slack beyond
+                        // ceil(WIDTH / 32) for the case where the last field ends on a
+                        // word boundary.
+                        flitBits[++index] = (offset == 0) ? 0U : (value >> (32 - offset));
+
                         offset = rem;
+                    }
+                    else
+                    {
+                        offset += width;
                     }
                 }
 
